Version 9.3.2

*  [Bryan Christ] A dialog repaints with one redraw.

   libviper 11.1.0 has a window or popup repaint everything it holds.
   vwm no longer redraws each label, button, list and box of a dialog
   by hand, innermost first, before the window around them: 253 of its
   333 redraw calls are gone, from the Settings, Hotkeys, Apps and
   Desktop dialogs, the screenshot and print dialogs, the shared
   popups, the menus, the calendar and the panel.  What is on screen
   is unchanged.

   Needs libviper 11.1.0.  STYLE_GUIDE.md describes the one-redraw
   rule and drops the old innermost-first instructions.

Version 9.3.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Uses libviper 11's names.

   libviper 11 renamed two operations: painting a widget's canvas is
   "redraw" (was "update") and copying the canvas to the screen is
   "blit" (was "draw").  vwm now calls vk_<type>_redraw() and
   vk_widget_blit() throughout.  The old names were still accepted, so
   nothing changes in how vwm behaves.

   Needs libviper 11.0.0 (libvdk.so.11).  STYLE_GUIDE.md uses the new
   names in its examples.

Version 9.3.0

*  [Bryan Christ] The screen can be published for a viewer.

   This is the session's half of a remote view: a second program on
   the same host (reached over ssh) showing what is on this screen in
   a terminal of its own, without the session moving there.  The
   viewer itself is not part of this release.

   -  New control op "view".  The reply gives the path of a file that
      holds the composed screen as libviper cells (character, RGB
      colours, bold / underline) and the screen's size.  The connection
      is then kept: each byte vwm sends on it means "the file has
      changed", and the viewer closing it is how it leaves.
   -  While at least one viewer is connected, vwm brings the file up to
      date at most twenty times a second and writes only the rows that
      changed.  With no viewer connected none of it runs and there is
      no file.
   -  Nothing waits for a viewer.  What vwm sends one is a single byte,
      never blocking, dropped if the viewer has not read the last; the
      screen's contents go through the file, which the viewer reads for
      itself.  A viewer that is slow or stuck cannot hold the screen
      up.
   -  The file is written in place with ordinary writes and read with
      ordinary reads; nothing is mapped into memory.  A counter in its
      header, odd while rows are being written, lets a reader tell a
      whole screen from half of two.  Its writer and its reader are
      both in viewfile.c, so vwm and a viewer cannot disagree about the
      layout.  libviper supplies only the cells.
   -  The file is in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (else ~/.config/vwm), readable by
      its owner alone, and removed when the last viewer leaves or the
      session ends.  Up to four viewers at once.  It works the same
      when the session is detached.
   -  "ping" reports how many viewers are connected ("viewers").
   -  vwm's first headless test, test_viewfile, run by ctest.

Version 9.2.0

*  [Bryan Christ] More than one session: --sid.

   -  "vwm --sid ID" starts a session with a name, beside any other
      that is running.  "vwm-resume --sid ID", "vwm-stop --sid ID" and
      "vwm-msg --sid ID <op>" act on that one.  An id is letters,
      digits, '-' and '_', up to 32 of them.
   -  With no --sid everything means the default session, id 0, exactly
      as before; "--sid 0" names it outright.
   -  Inside a session the commands mean that session without being
      told: a session has always passed its control socket's path to
      the programs it runs.  An explicit --sid wins over that, which is
      how a terminal in one session reaches another.
   -  A session is still nothing more than its control socket.  The
      default's is ~/.config/vwm/control.sock; a named one's is
      control-ID.sock beside it.  --sid sets VWM_CONTROL_SOCK to that
      path and everything else carries on as it did (session.c).
   -  The messages that tell you what to type include the id: "vwm:
      detached ... vwm-resume --sid work returns to it".
   -  vwm-resume and vwm-stop now refuse an argument they do not know
      and answer --help.  They used to ignore arguments.
   -  All sessions share one settings file.

Version 9.1.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Screenshots are drawn from libviper's portable cells.

   -  libviper 10.1 can read any canvas back as cells that mean
      something outside the process: the character as Unicode, both
      colours as RGB, reverse and dim already worked into the colours
      (vdk_cells_read_row).  The screenshot's capture is now a loop
      over that, and its renderer draws the colours it is given
      without asking ncurses anything.  The line-drawing table, the
      palette and the per-cell decode that vwm carried are gone.
   -  A screenshot of ordinary content is the same, pixel for pixel,
      as before.
   -  Fixed, as a result: a double-width character (CJK, some emoji)
      was drawn once in each of its two cells; it is drawn once across
      both.  A colour a program had redefined was drawn as the standard
      palette's colour of that number; it is drawn as redefined.  A
      colour pair numbered above 32767 was misread.
   -  Needs libviper 10.1.0; the build stops with a message saying so
      on anything older.

Version 9.1.0

*  [Bryan Christ] A terminal window shows the title its program sets.

   -  A program that names its window (the xterm sequences OSC 0 and
      OSC 2: shells, editors, ssh, most full-screen tools) now has that
      name shown to the right of the app's own, as in
      " Claude Code: fixing the tests ".  Before, the title a program
      asked for went nowhere.
   -  The program's part is cut to what the border has room for, with
      "..." where it was cut, and refitted when the window changes
      width.  On a window too narrow for it the app's name stands
      alone.  A program that sets an empty title gets the app's name
      back.  Control characters in a title are left out.
   -  "vwm-msg list-windows", the Windows menu and Manage Desktop show
      the same title the border does.  Scripts that matched a window by
      an exact title should match on how it starts instead: a shell
      whose prompt sets the title makes it
      " <app>: user@host: dir ".
   -  Needs libvtty 11.2, which reports the title; the build stops
      with a message saying so on anything older.
   -  Fixed: a double-width character (CJK, some emoji) on a window's
      top border was repeated along the rest of the border, over the
      buttons.  The focus colouring read each border cell out and wrote
      it back, which split such a character in two.  It now changes the
      cells' colours only.  Titles could not carry such characters
      before, so this was not reachable.
   -  Manage Desktop no longer cuts a long title by bytes, which could
      split a multi-byte character; the list clips by screen column.

Version 9.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm builds against libvtty, the new name of libvterm.

   -  libvterm was renamed libvtty in 11.0 to end a name collision with
      an unrelated library.  Its header is now vtty.h, it links as
      -lvtty, and its functions, types and macros start with vtty_ and
      VTTY_.  vwm follows: it needs libvtty 11.0 or newer and no longer
      builds against libvterm.
   -  cmake/FindVTERM.cmake is replaced by cmake/FindVTTY.cmake, which
      sets VTTY_LIBRARY and VTTY_INCLUDE_DIR.
   -  The terminal module loads libvtty.so.
   -  Nothing a user or a script sees is renamed.  The terminal profiles
      (vterm-color, vterm-xterm256 and the rest), the VTerm window
      titles, the "vterm" kind in vwm-msg replies and the settings file
      are all unchanged.
   -  To upgrade: install libvtty, remove the old libvterm by hand
      (libvterm.so, libvterm.a, vterm.h, libvterm.pc), then build and
      install vwm.

Version 8.1.10

*  [Bryan Christ] Window-manager mode works again, and the last of the
   simplification list.

   -  Fixed: window-manager mode has been dead since 8.0.0.  Alt+w (and
      VWM > Manage windows) did nothing, so the keys that move, resize,
      cycle and close windows in that mode could not be reached.  The
      block of the key handler that did all this was removed by
      mistake along with the Teleport prompt.  It is back.
   -  The Ctrl-arrow keys that resize in window-manager mode were
      matched against fixed key numbers, which ncurses assigns
      differently from version to version; they did not match on
      current ncurses.  The code is now looked up from the sequence
      the key sends, with the old numbers as a fallback.
   -  The eight window move / resize functions (one per direction) are
      two: vwm_window_nudge and vwm_window_grow.  (TODO S6)
   -  Terminals: the wheel and Alt+PgUp / Alt+PgDn each had their own
      copy of "move the scroll position, clamp it, repaint"; they share
      vwmterm_scroll_by.  The six terminal kinds were registered by six
      copies of one block and are now a table.  The selection's
      start-before-end ordering, written out twice, is
      vwmterm_selection_span.  (TODO S12)
   -  Manage Apps, Hotkeys and Settings each exported five or six
      functions handing out their popups so that the mouse code could
      test them one by one.  Each now exports one hit test,
      vwm_manage_*_hit_test, and seventeen accessors are gone, with one
      in Manage Desktop that nothing called.  (TODO 13)

Version 8.1.9

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Hotkeys and Manage Apps: less repeated code,
   same behaviour.

   -  Manage Hotkeys named all fourteen hotkeys three times over: once
      to load them from vwm, once to apply them, once to see whether
      anything had changed.  The table of hotkeys now carries where
      each one lives in vwm, and the three are loops over it.
      (TODO S4)
   -  In both dialogs the Up and Down arrows were the same block
      written twice, as were wheel-up and wheel-down, and the "which
      button was it" switch was written once for the keyboard and once
      for the mouse.  Each is now one function (hotkey_list_step,
      app_list_step, button_activate).  (TODO S4, S5)
   -  Manage Apps paired its four dropdowns with their focus zones in
      a dozen places.  zone_dropdown is now the one place, and a click
      on any of the four goes through dropdown_zone_open.  (TODO S5)
   -  Manage Apps: two includes it no longer used (strings.h,
      winman.h) and two sizes it asked for and never read are gone.
      (TODO S5)

Version 8.1.8

*  [Bryan Christ] Menus: less code, and one resize fix.

   -  Closing a dropdown, the Apps submenu or the calendar popup took
      the widgets apart by hand: free the scroll bar, unhook the child,
      destroy the child, destroy the window.  Since libviper 10 a
      window owns what it holds and a widget its scroll bar, so each
      is now one vk_window_destroy.  vwm_menu_free_scroller is gone.
      A terminal no longer destroys its own scroll bar on close; it
      goes with the window.  (TODO S16)
   -  The four dropdown builders (VWM, Apps, a category's apps,
      Windows) each set up the same list, did the same sizing and made
      the same bordered window, and two built the same scroll bar.
      They now share menu_list_create, menu_window_create and
      menu_list_add_scroller.  (TODO S9)
   -  "Close the dropdown and take the focus off the bar" was written
      out in eight places and is now menu_dismiss; the Left / Right
      walk along the bar, written twice, is menubar_step; the three
      menu-bar callbacks that differed only in a number are one
      vwm_menu_activate.  (TODO S9)
   -  Fixed: a dropdown that was open while the terminal changed size
      came back four columns narrow, clipping its longer rows
      ("Developm", "Messagin").  The resize worked the size out again
      by itself and left out the padding the builders add.  Building
      and resizing now share menu_list_fit.
   -  A dropdown's title is now never clipped by a list narrower than
      it.  Only the Windows dropdown guarded against that before; the
      VWM and Apps lists were always wide enough in practice.

Version 8.1.7

*  [Bryan Christ] Three small fixes around Manage Desktop and the
   status bar.

   -  Manage Desktop's list now follows the desktop while the dialog is
      open.  A window that closed, opened, was minimized or was
      restored behind the dialog (a program exiting, vwm-msg) used to
      leave the list as it was until the dialog was reopened.  The
      list is rebuilt at each such change, and the ticks and the
      cursor stay on the windows they were on.  (TODO D21)
   -  The "Move To..." popup took a click on its bottom border as a
      press of Okay or Cancel, and ignored a click on the top row of
      either button.  The button rows are now the three the buttons
      occupy.  (TODO D20)
   -  The status bar of an empty desktop read "Alt ~ Menu | Alt d
      Switch Desktop" at startup and "Press Alt ~ for Menu" once any
      dialog or the last window had closed.  It is the startup text
      throughout.  The choice between it and the window key help was
      written out in nine places; they now call one
      vwm_panel_status_idle.  (TODO D22)

      D22 was recorded as "the status bar keeps a dialog's key help
      after the dialog closes".  That did not reproduce: the text is
      restored in the same refresh that removes the dialog.  What was
      seen was the dialog itself staying up for the second ncurses
      waits after a bare Esc.

