   instead of repeating the loop that finalize runs.  (TODO S13)

Version 8.1.3

*  [Bryan Christ] The remaining defects from the review list.

   -  Settings > Load reads the per-desktop colours and wallpapers.
      It skipped the three desktop_* arrays, so those rows kept the
      running values and a Save after the Load wrote them over the
      file's.  (TODO D6)
   -  Settings: Left / Right work on the Copy to Clipboard and
      Desktop Wallpaper rows.  They did nothing there but still marked
      the dialog as changed, which brought up "Discard changes?" on
      the way out.  The dialog is now marked changed only when a value
      actually changes.  (TODO D7)
   -  Manage Apps, Hotkeys and Settings are modal for the mouse too.
      A click beside the open dialog reached the window behind it,
      raising it and typing into it.  Such clicks are now swallowed;
      the panel and status bar still answer.  (TODO D11)
   -  Manage Apps and Manage Hotkeys find the button under a click
      from where the buttons are (new vwm_button_bar_hit).  The column
      table they used had Save / Load / Close one column to the left,
      so clicking Load's right edge closed the dialog.  (TODO D5)
   -  A window narrower than 9 columns has no title-bar buttons, drawn
      or clickable.  On a 5-to-8-column window they were drawn over
      the left corner and a click there minimized or closed it.
      (TODO D12)
   -  The menubar is sized from the "(N) Windows" label, so a
      three-digit count is not clipped.  (TODO D16)

Version 8.1.2

*  [Bryan Christ] A module cloned for one launch is freed after it.
   `vwm-msg launch` and the screensaver each clone a terminal profile,
   configure it with the program to run, start it, and then dropped
   the clone: about 1KB per control-socket launch and per screensaver
   start.  New vwm_module_destroy(), with a destroy hook in the module
   struct so a module type frees what it owns (the terminal module's
   program path and argument list).  A terminal takes what it needs
   from its module while starting and does not look at it again.

   With libvterm 10.10.2, which frees a terminal's command line on
   destroy, opening and closing terminals leaves nothing behind.  This
   closes TODO M3; M2 was dropped as no longer reproducible.

Version 8.1.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Three small correctness fixes from the review list.

   -  The shutdown flag is a volatile sig_atomic_t.  It is written from
      the SIGTERM handler and was a plain int.  New type name
      vwm_shutdown_t (sched.h), used by the flag, every extern of it
      and every pointer to it, so they cannot drift apart.  (TODO D17)
   -  The debug crash handler only redirects stdin when it managed to
      open the terminal.  A stray semicolon after the test made the
      dup2 unconditional.  _DEBUG builds only.  (TODO D18)
   -  The numeric fields in Settings test for a digit with a plain
      range check.  The keystroke can be a key code above 255 (an
      arrow, a mouse event), and isdigit() is only defined for
      unsigned char values.  (TODO D19)

Version 8.1.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Built on libviper 10, where a container owns what it
   holds: destroying a window destroys everything inside it.  vwm
   relied on that being true long before it was, so this release is
   mostly leaks that stop:

   -  A closed terminal window is freed.  vwm destroyed it through
      vk_widget_destroy(), which until libviper 10 did nothing for a
      window: the window object, its content widget, its event
      handlers and its ncurses windows stayed allocated for the life
      of the session, for every terminal ever closed.  The print and
      screenshot tool windows were the same.
   -  The four management dialogs free their widgets on close.  Each
      destroyed only its outer window and left every button, box,
      list, frame and label inside it behind: about 3.6KB per open of
      Manage Desktop, 5KB for Settings and Manage Hotkeys, 18KB for
      Manage Apps Menu, not counting their ncurses windows.
   -  The popups inside those dialogs free their contents too.
   -  Manage Desktop's Confirm Close and Move popups are destroyed at
      all: they were handed to vk_window_destroy(), which refuses a
      popup, so neither was ever freed.

   The only source changes are those two popup calls and dropping the
   hand-written scroller teardown in Manage Desktop that 8.0.5 added,
   which the library now does.  This was the bulk of TODO M3.

   Needs libviper 10.0.0 (libvdk.so.10).

Version 8.0.5

*  [Bryan Christ] Two scroll bars are no longer leaked.  A scroller
   attached to a widget is not owned by it, so destroying the widget
   leaves the scroller behind.  Manage Desktop leaked its list's
   scroller on every close, and the Apps dropdown (and a category
   submenu long enough to scroll) leaked one on every menu close.
   Each is now detached and destroyed before its list goes.
   (TODO D8, D9)

Version 8.0.4

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Desktop acts on the windows its rows show.
   Each checked row was mapped to the window at the same position in
   the deck, but a window that closes on its own while the dialog is
   open moves every later window up one position.  Close, Move,
   Minimize and Restore then landed on the wrong window, or on none.
   Rows now remember the id of the window they were listed for; a row
   whose window is gone is skipped.  (TODO D10)

*  [Bryan Christ] Copying a selection checks its allocation.  The
   buffer is sized for the worst case (hundreds of KB for a large
   selection) and was written to without a NULL check; a selection
   that clamps to nothing no longer computes a size from a negative
   row count either.  (TODO D15)

Version 8.0.3

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Desktop: the Confirm Close popup only answers
   on its buttons.  The click row was ignored, so a click anywhere in
   the popup's left half -- the title, the message text, or the release
   at the end of dragging the popup by its title -- counted as "Yes"
   and closed the checked windows.  Only the three rows of the button
   bar answer now.  (TODO D4)

*  [Bryan Christ] Settings: Load resets the selection.  After loading
   a file the highlight went back to the first row but the dialog's
   own record of the selected row did not, so Modify opened the
   setting that had been selected before the load.  (TODO D2)

Version 8.0.2

*  [Bryan Christ] Big-font text is drawn with square pixels.  The
   vwmfont module mapped one font pixel to one terminal cell, and a
   cell is about twice as tall as it is wide, so every glyph came out
   stretched to twice its height.  Each pixel is now two cells wide
   and one tall, which keeps the font's own proportions.  Big text is
   therefore twice as wide as before: a 9-letter host name at 8x16
   takes 144 columns, and what does not fit is clipped on the right as
   it always was.

*  [Bryan Christ] The cached big-font host name is forgotten when the
   screen is rebuilt on another terminal.  It was kept across the
   move, so its ncurses window belonged to the screen left behind, and
   the next change of font, fill or host name freed a window of a
   dead screen -- the corruption the wallpaper cache's orphan path
   already avoids.  Since 8.0.0 the screen is rebuilt on every attach,
   detach and resume, so the cache was stale from the first attach.
   New vwm_hostname_cache_orphan(), called from vwm_on_teleport.
   (TODO D14)

Version 8.0.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix a crash from the Windows menu.  Its rows held
   the address of each window, and the menu can stay open while a
   listed window goes away -- a terminal closes itself when its
   program exits.  Choosing that row then handed freed memory to
   vwm_restore_window and took the session down.  Rows now carry the
   window's id, which is looked up again when the row is chosen; a
   window that is gone is not found.  (TODO D1)

*  [Bryan Christ] Closing or minimizing a window cancels a move or
   resize drag in flight on it.  Terminals already did this from their
   own close handler; any other window closed mid-drag left the drag
   pointing at freed memory for the next mouse event.  (TODO D13)

Version 8.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm is durable on its own; dtach is retired.

   The session runs as a background process -- its own session, no
   controlling terminal -- apart from whatever terminal it is shown
   on.  `vwm` starts it and then attaches the terminal it was run
   from; what stays in the shell's foreground is a small client
   (attach.c) that waits until the session leaves that terminal.
   vwm-resume runs the same client through the new `vwm-msg attach`.

   -  A terminal that goes away (hang-up, closed window, dropped SSH)
      no longer takes the session with it, the starting terminal
      included: vwm lets go of it and runs headless until resumed.
      This also fixes 7.2.0 spinning at 100% CPU when a moved
      session's terminal hung up.
   -  Detach on demand: Ctrl-\ (hotkey "detach", rebindable), VWM >
      Detach, `vwm-msg detach`.
   -  vwm-stop ends a session from any terminal, attached or not
      (control op `stop`): every program is hung up, then vwm exits.
   -  Closing a terminal window ends its program with SIGHUP, then
      SIGKILL after 300ms if it is still there, instead of SIGKILL at
      once -- shells get to save their history.
   -  Startup problems are reported on the launching terminal: too
      small, a session already running, or a start that failed in the
      background.  Run from no terminal, vwm starts the session 80x25
      with nothing attached.
   -  Control socket: new ops attach (keeps its connection; a line on
      it releases the client, its closing releases the terminal),
      detach and stop.  ping reports pid and headless.  The adopt op
      and ping's dtach field are gone.

   Removed: running under dtach (vwm-start is now another name for
   vwm; VWM_SOCK, the held adopt, the same-size reattach SIGWINCH
   handler, the wait for a 0x0 pty to get a size and the status bar's
   dtach indicator all went with it), the home terminal, "Teleport
   home", "Teleport to..." and its prompt, the table of known
   terminals, and suspending the shell of a terminal vwm moves onto.
   A session shows on one terminal at a time; dtach's mirroring is
   not replaced.

   Needs libviper 9.0.0 (vk_screen_detach, vk_screen_create_detached,
   adopt without eviction).

Version 7.2.0

*  [Bryan Christ] The scheduler is an event loop.  Tasks wait
   (vwm_sched_wait) instead of being polled in turn: a terminal with
   nothing to read, the input task with no key, and the clock between
   ticks are not run at all.  The scheduler sleeps in one ppoll() on
   every wake descriptor -- the keyboard, the gpm socket, each pty,
   the control socket -- and wakes the task a readable one belongs to.
   A heartbeat wakes every task once per tick, and whenever a signal
   interrupts the sleep, so anything without a descriptor (a child
   that exited, a key pushed back inside ncurses) is noticed within
   100ms; vwm_sched_wake() and vwm_input_wake() make the common cases
   immediate.

   Removed with the polling: the per-task did_work flags, the slot
   reference bits, the sweep counter and the "bonus run", and the
   SIGIO wake-ups on the keyboard, the mouse and every pty (the
   terminal module's signals.c is gone).  The keyboard descriptor is
   now the screen's own, so it follows a teleport or adopt.  Needs
   libviper 8.1.0 (VK_KMIO_NOWAIT, vk_kmio_gpm_fd,
   vk_screen_get_input_fd).

Version 7.1.0

*  [Bryan Christ] The scheduler waits on every terminal's pty itself.
   A terminal task used to wait up to 10ms for data on each of its
   turns, so every idle terminal put up to 10ms in front of the
   keyboard and of any terminal that did have output.  The task now
   takes what is there and returns, and registers its pty with the
   scheduler, whose one ppoll() ends as soon as any of them is
   readable (vwm_sched_wake_fd_add / _del).  A terminal frozen for
   SELECT mode takes its pty out while it is not reading.

   Measured in a pty, one busy terminal plus idle ones (before -> after):
     key echo, median      alone 11ms -> 1ms;  8 idle  37ms -> 1ms
     30 fps animation      8 idle  41% of frames shown -> 100%
     4 MB of output        8 idle  24s -> 0.4s
   Idle CPU is unchanged (about 1%).

Version 7.0.2

*  [Bryan Christ] "make install" runs ldconfig on Linux.  vwm installs
   no library the linker cache serves, but it needs libvdk.so.N and
   libvterm.so, and one of those installed just before without ldconfig
   left the new vwm unable to start.  Skipped when DESTDIR is set; a
   failure to write the cache is a warning, not a failed install.
   README: INSTALLATION corrected (install locations, the cmake command).

Version 7.0.1

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm no longer looks for or links libgpm.  libviper
   8.0.0 talks to the gpm daemon itself, so the console mouse needs
   nothing at build time; the gpm daemon is still what provides it at
   run time.  cmake/FindGPM.cmake is removed.  Needs libviper 8.0.0.

Version 7.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Major version.  No code change from 6.5.0; the work
   that landed there changes how a session is used and is worth the
   number.  A session is no longer tied to the terminal, or the kind
   of terminal, it was started on:

   -  vwm-resume brings any running session -- dtach or not -- to the
      terminal it is run from, and vwm adapts to that terminal: type,
      mouse (xterm reporting or GPM), console pointer, UTF-8 or ASCII
      glyphs.
   -  Resuming a dtach session takes it away from other terminals
      instead of mirroring it.
   -  The VWM menu has "Teleport home" and "Teleport to..." in place of
      "Teleport".
   -  New control operation "adopt"; ping reports "dtach".

   Changes in behaviour since 6.4.x that a script or habit may rely on:
   vwm-resume detaches other attached terminals; vwm-resume on a
   session started with plain vwm moves it rather than failing; the
   menu entry "Teleport" is gone under that name; building needs
   libviper 7.10.0; and (since 6.4.2) the vwm_panel_message_* functions
   no longer exist for modules.

*  [Bryan Christ] README: new section MOVING A SESSION; the Teleport,
   control socket and remote session text brought up to date.

Version 6.5.0

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-resume brings any running session to the terminal
   it is run from, and tells vwm what that terminal is.  New control
   operation "adopt" (vwm-msg adopt [--tty PATH] [--term TYPE]) carries
   the tty and its terminal type.  A session started directly moves its
   screen there at once, as Teleport does.  A dtach session keeps its
   pty and holds the description until the client reattaches, then
   rebuilds its screen in place for the new type.  In both cases TERM
   and the GPM console number follow the terminal, the GPM connection
   is reopened, and the console mouse pointer is switched on or off to
   match.  Needs libviper 7.10.0 (vk_screen_adopt, vk_kmio_gpm_reset).

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-resume detaches the session from any other
   terminal before attaching.  dtach mirrors a session to every
   attached client, so resuming without detaching first left the old
   terminal live alongside the new one.  The terminal that loses
   the session is told it moved, instead of the "Could not attach"
   error that dtach's exit status used to trigger.

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-msg ping reports whether the session runs under
   dtach ("dtach": true/false).

*  [Bryan Christ] adopt is refused from a terminal inside the session
   ("inside this session"); vwm-resume says so instead of nesting.

*  [Bryan Christ] The menu Teleport goes through the same path.  A
   typed device path cannot say what kind of terminal it is, so
   /dev/ttyN is taken as a Linux console, a terminal the session has
   been on before keeps the type it had, and an unknown terminal
   reached from a console borrows the type of the last non-console
   one.

*  [Bryan Christ] New menu entry "Teleport home" returns the session to
   the terminal vwm was started on, as the type it had then.  That
   terminal's shell is blocked behind vwm and cannot call the session
   back with vwm-resume.  The existing entry is renamed "Teleport
   to...".  Both stay hidden under dtach.  While the session is
   already home, "Teleport home" is shown grayed out and cannot be
   selected: the arrow keys step over it and a click does nothing
   (libviper 7.10.0 inactive list items).

*  [Bryan Christ] The "Teleport to:" prompt shows "Esc to cancel" in
   gray while nothing has been typed.

*  [Bryan Christ] The look follows the terminal too.  The UTF-8 or
   ASCII choice was made once at startup; it is now asked when
   drawing, and the panel's activity indicator and dtach dot are
   re-picked after a move.  A session started in an X terminal gets
   the console forms on the console (spinner, "o", "v", ">", Stiple in
   place of the brick wallpapers), and one started on the console gets
   the UTF-8 forms in an X terminal.

Version 6.4.4

*  [Bryan Christ] The VWM menu does not offer Teleport in a dtach
   session (one started with vwm-start).  Teleport moves vwm's screen
   to another tty, but the dtach client stays attached to the pty left
   behind, so the session could not be brought back.  Started directly,
   vwm still offers it.

Version 6.4.3

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-start exports VK_GPM_VC on a Linux virtual
   console.  Under dtach vwm sits on a pty, so libgpm asked gpm for
   console 0 and the daemon refused the connection: no GPM mouse.
   vwm-start now records the console it was started on and libviper
   7.9.0 passes it to gpm.  Off the console the variable is left unset
   and nothing changes.  vwm-resume from a different console is not
   handled: the session keeps the console it was started on.

*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-start no longer dies with "[EOF - dtach
   terminating]" on a text console.  Under dtach the pty is 0x0 until
   the client attaches, and the client clears the screen first, which is
   slow on a framebuffer console; vwm's minimum-size check ran in that
   gap, read 0x0, and exited before anything could show the message.
   The check now waits up to two seconds for a size, and skips itself if
   none arrives.

Version 6.4.2

*  [Bryan Christ] Removed the menu-glyph label from the left end of the
   top panel.  Its glyph, U+2630, is two columns wide to glibc 2.43
   (Unicode 16) but one column wide to VTE, so ncurses and the terminal
   disagreed on the cursor position and two cells of the panel were left
   unpainted (black) on Ubuntu 26.04.  The label was also the only place
   panel messages were shown, so the unused vwm_panel_message_* API
   (add, del, find, touch, promote) is removed with it.

Version 6.4.1

*  [Bryan Christ] The vwm-msg control socket survives a stray second
   vwm.  vwm now answers --help/--version and exits before ncurses, so
   a piped probe no longer starts a window manager that unlinks the live
   session's socket.  vwm refuses to start when another session is
   already listening, ctl_init never unlinks a live socket (only a stale
   one), the listen and accepted fds are close-on-exec so child
   terminals cannot inherit or later unlink them, and shutdown removes
   the socket path only when its inode is still the one this process
   bound.

Version 6.4.0

*  [Bryan Christ] The Apps menu groups launchers by category.  The Apps
   dropdown lists one row per category that has visible apps (Tool,
   System, Game, ...), each marked with a submenu arrow; the highlighted
   category's apps open in a second menu beside that row.  Hovering a
   category -- with the arrow keys or the mouse -- opens its submenu;
   Right, Enter or a click moves into it, Left or Esc comes back, and Esc
   again (or the menu hotkey) closes both.  The submenu opens to the
   right of the dropdown, or to its left when it would run off the
   screen, with its first app on the category's row.  The arrow is "▸",
   or ">" when the terminal cannot show UTF-8.  Needs libviper 7.8.0
   (vk_listbox_set_item_submenu).

Version 6.1.4

*  [Bryan Christ] CMake project() is C-only (LANGUAGES C).  A fresh
   configure no longer requires a C++ compiler; vwm has no C++ sources.

Version 6.1.3

*  [Bryan Christ] Control socket I/O is bounded and complete.  Accepted
   clients get a 2s SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO so a connect-and-silence
   cannot freeze the render loop; ctl_reply write-all retries EINTR
   and only appends the newline if the JSON body fully landed.  Fixes
   GitHub issue #89.

Version 6.1.2

*  [Bryan Christ] vwmterm init no longer treats a missing unversioned
   libutil.so as fatal (glibc 2.34+ folded libutil into libc and
   distros dropped that symlink).  Tries libutil.so, then
   libutil.so.1, then accepts a libc that already provides forkpty.
   Fixes GitHub issue #88.

Version 6.1.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Desktop -> Move -> Reorient (was Home
   coordinates) now runs the same on-screen fit as restore: pull the
   origin back, then shrink if the window is still clipped.  A window
   hanging off the right or bottom edge can be recovered from the
   picker, not only by minimize/restore.
*  [Bryan Christ] vwm-msg launch / launch-app run that same fit after
   spawn so a requested size cannot hang off the screen.  vwm-msg
   resize clamps width/height to the remaining space from the window's
   current origin (right edge on-screen, bottom above the status row).

Version 6.1.0

*  [Bryan Christ] New windows cascade +2 columns and +2 rows when they
   would share another visible member's origin on the same deck, so a
   second VTerm Color does not sit exactly on top of the first.
   Fullscreen windows are left alone.
*  [Bryan Christ] Attention flash: vwm-msg attention <id> (or launch
   --attention) switches to that window and pulses its border about
   every 1/2 s (white-on-red / black-on-yellow) until the user clicks
   or types in it, it is closed or minimized, or attention-off.
   list-windows reports "attention".

Version 6.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Control plane: Unix socket (VWM_CONTROL_SOCK, else
   ~/.config/vwm/control.sock) plus vwm-msg.  SO_PEERCRED same-uid
   only.  Ops: ping, list-windows / list-desktops / focused, launch /
   launch-app, list-apps, focus, close, minimize, restore, move,
   resize, desktop, send-keys, capture, screenshot.  launch-app joins
   remaining words as the title (so unquoted VTerm Color works).
   send-keys --key F1..F12 writes the xterm CSI/SS3 sequence, not
   ncurses KEY_F(n) (which libvterm could pack as two garbage bytes).
   Window ids come from libviper 7.1 vk_widget_get_id().
   Requires libviper 7.2.0+ (host bracketed paste so an xfce/VTE
   paste is one blob, not a stream of Enters).
*  [Bryan Christ] Host paste (parent terminal) and vwmterm middle-click /
   Alt+Shift+V use vterm_write_data so a child that armed DECSET 2004
   (Grok, vim, …) treats the payload as paste, not typed Enter.

Version 5.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Screen capture is a core API, not a loadable module.
   vwm_screenshot_save(target, path) writes a PNG of the composed
   desktop or the focused window.  The VWM-menu dialog is now a
   caller of that function (same Entire screen / Top window options).
   Remove any leftover libvwmscrshot.so from the module directory
   after upgrade -- it is no longer built or installed.
*  [Bryan Christ] Screenshots rasterize with DejaVu Sans Mono (system
   copy, then the bundled fonts/ pair).  Override at configure time
   with -DVWM_SCREENSHOT_FONT= and -DVWM_SCREENSHOT_FONT_BOLD=.

Version 4.9.9

*  [Bryan Christ] Child OSC 52 SET (vim, Grok, tmux, Claude Code, …)
   now feeds the same clipboard as SELECT-mode copy.  libvterm 10.9
   delivers VTERM_EVENT_CLIPBOARD; vwmterm stores the payload for
   middle-click / Alt+Shift+V and honors Settings clipboard_mode
   (Never / OSC 52 / xclip / Both) for the host hop.  Pc p/s goes to
   X PRIMARY; everything else to CLIPBOARD.  Empty Pd clears.
   Requires libvterm 10.9+.

Version 4.9.8

*  [Bryan Christ] Menubar dropdowns now scroll with the mouse wheel (Apps, VWM,
   and Minimized menus).  The wheel drives a scroller nudge (vk_scroller_nudge)
   when an attached scrollbar is available — requiring libvdk (libviper) 7.0+;
   the Apps menu registers vk_listbox_scroll_apply as the scroll handler -- and
   falls back to set_scroll_pos for VWM / Minimized menus that do not attach a
   scroller, so every dropdown pans its viewable region without moving the
   selection; keyboard still moves the highlight.  Scrollbar thumb tracks
   scroll_pos rather than curr_item.

Version 4.9.7

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Apps can set a per-app default terminal size
   (width x height in character cells, default 80x25).  The Category row
   now shares space with Width and Height spinbuttons; values persist as
   "width" / "height" on each programs[] entry and are applied when a
   non-fullscreen vwmterm launches (clamped so the window still fits the
   host screen).

Version 4.9.6

*  [Bryan Christ] The screen saver now tracks a terminal resize.  While the
   saver (a fullscreen vterm running the configured lock program) is up,
   poll_input_thd previously swallowed KEY_RESIZE, so a dtach reattach onto a
   different-size terminal left the saver -- and the locked program inside it --
   stuck at the old geometry (e.g. vlock not filling a grown screen).  A resize
   is now forwarded to the saver overlay only: vwm_screensaver_resize() resizes
   the fullscreen vterm window (clearing its VK_STATE_NORESIZE for the
   programmatic resize and growing its content child), which drives
   vterm_resize() -> libvterm TIOCSWINSZ + SIGWINCH into the locked program so
   it repaints at the new size.  The hidden desktop underneath is never
   repainted, so a resize can't expose it.

Version 4.9.5

*  [Bryan Christ] Selecting/copying text while scrolled back into vwmterm
   history no longer snaps the view to the live screen.  The selection render
   now draws at the current scroll position (vwmterm_scroll_render) instead of
   always the live buffer, so the view stays put and the anchor lands on the
   row you clicked; and the copy reads the composed scrollback view via
   libvterm's new vterm_copy_scrollback() rather than the live-only
   vterm_copy_buffer, so the copied text is the history you selected.  Requires
   libvterm 10.7+.

Version 4.9.4

*  [Bryan Christ] Fit an over-large window back on screen after a shrink -- a
   dtach/abduco reattach or a teleport onto a smaller terminal, or restoring a
   minimized window when the screen has shrunk.  Two steps: pull the top-left
   back to the home corner, then, if the window is still larger than the usable
   area, resize it down by the minimum needed to fit.  Previously such a window
   kept its old size and stayed clipped.  New shared helper
   vwm_fit_window_onscreen(), used by both the KEY_RESIZE clamp and the
   minimized-restore path.

Version 4.9.3

*  [Bryan Christ] Track libviper 6.0.0's relief-API rename: the box-style
   setters vk_button/dropdown/spinbutton/input_set_relief_style became
   vk_*_set_border_style.  Updated at every call site (manage dialogs, the
   screenshot module).  No behaviour change.
*  [Bryan Christ] The scrollback spinner's arrow box is now flat.  A tee cell
   is shared by two regions and cannot satisfy two relief directions, so the
   raised arrow buttons' lit edges met the sunken value field's dark ones at
   the tees and read as glitches; the sunken field now carries the 3D and the
   arrows sit in a plain box.  Pairs with libviper 6.0.0's centralised relief
   drawing (which also fixes the manage-dialog dropdowns and the file dialog's
   input/frame shading).  Requires libviper 6.0.0+.

Version 4.9.2

*  [Bryan Christ] Desktop backgrounds now render bright and 256-colour choices
   correctly.  The surface background was handed to libviper as a chtype, whose
   pair went through COLOR_PAIR() and truncated past 255 -- so a bright desktop
   colour showed the wrong colour in the cells the terminal's hardware-scroll
   optimisation exposes.  Adopts libviper 5.6.0's widened
   vk_screen_set_surface_bkgd(wch, attrs, pair).  Requires libviper 5.6.0+.

Version 4.9.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Window title-bar controls now render as [v][X] -- the
   minimize control is bracketed to match close -- and the pair is inset two
   columns from the top-right corner rather than flush against it.  Each
   button now has a full three-column click target (minimize was one column).

Version 4.9.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Minimized windows.  A window's title bar gains a minimize
   glyph (a down-arrow, "v" on non-UTF-8) left of [X] that hides it in place;
   the top panel shows a "(N) Minimized" pulldown, next to VWM, that lists the
   hidden windows and restores one on click.  The Alt+W manage-windows tool
   gains Minimize and Restore buttons that act on the checked windows and
   flags already-minimized rows with the same down-arrow.  Restoring a window
   raises it and returns focus.  Requires libviper 5.5.0+.
*  [Bryan Christ] Window focus decoration is now driven by libviper's
   VK_EVENT_ON_FINALIZE deck event.  A single handler repaints the focus
   borders whenever the deck's membership or stacking changes, replacing the
   hand-rolled "demote the old top, promote the new top" logic that was
   duplicated across raise, cycle, close, minimize, restore, cross-desktop
   move, and window spawn.  Border changes are immediate and can no longer
   fall out of sync.

Version 4.8.0

*  [Bryan Christ] vwmterm scrollback now reveals less than one screenful of
   history.  Scrolling back into a short capture -- fewer lines than the
   window is tall, e.g. a brief `ls -l` -- previously did nothing, because
   the render clamped to whole screens of evicted rows.  It now composes the
   scrolled-back history above the live screen (via libvterm's new
   vterm_wnd_scrollback), so the wheel, Alt+PgUp/PgDn, and the bar all reach
   the very first captured line the way a hardware terminal scrolls back.
   Requires libvterm 10.4+.

Version 4.7.0

*  [Bryan Christ] vwmterm scrollbar.  Bordered terminal windows show a
   vertical scrollbar down the right edge, built on libviper's vk_scroller.
   The thumb is proportional to the real scrollback and reaches the top at
   the oldest captured line; scrolling (wheel, Alt+PgUp/PgDn, and dragging
   or clicking the bar) is clamped to what has actually been captured, so it
   no longer runs past into blank rows.  On the alternate screen (vim, less,
   myman) there is no scrollback, so the bar parks at a full thumb and
   scrolling is disabled.  The bar's color tracks window focus, and thumb
   drags are captured by the input loop so a release is never missed.  The
   terminal keeps its full size -- the bar takes its own column and the
   frame size label reports the real dimensions.  Requires libvterm 10.3+
   and libviper 5.4.0+.

Version 4.6.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Big-font host name.  The bottom-left hostname label can
   now render as large "pixel-art" text from a Terminus PSF console font,
   via the new loadable vwmfont module (modules/vwmfont, built and loaded
   like vwmterm/vwmprint).  Settings add "Hostname Font" (Basic plus the
   nine Terminus grids) and "Hostname Fill" (Full Block / O / X); the
   existing Hostname Colors still apply.  Falls back to the plain one-row
   label when the font is Basic or the vwmfont module is not installed.
   No font package is required -- discovery uses the console-setup
   Terminus PSFs already present (Uni3/Uni2-Terminus*) and also matches
   upstream ter-*.psf.gz.

Version 4.5.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Settings: the Desktop color option now picks both a
   foreground and a background ("Desktop Colors ... [White/Blue]") via a
   dual-swatch Modify picker (Tab between the two swatches), instead of
   background only.  The chosen foreground is the wallpaper glyph color.

*  [Bryan Christ] Add the "Crosses" wallpaper -- a 2x6 staggered '+'
   pattern.

*  [Bryan Christ] Render bright (8-15) desktop foreground/background
   colors correctly.  The wallpaper renderers packed the color pair into
   an 8-bit chtype via COLOR_PAIR(), truncating pair numbers above 255
   (bright backgrounds 12-15) to the wrong color; they now apply the pair
   as a separate short via wattr_set.  Requires libviper 5.3.0, which
   carries the matching fix for the picker's own widgets.

Version 4.4.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Apps: add the "%fd" Params launch token.  An app
   whose Params contain %fd pops a file picker at launch (rooted at the
   home directory), substitutes the chosen file into the app's arguments,
   and starts it; cancelling the picker aborts the launch.  The Add/Edit
   App dialog labels the Params field with the token.

*  [Bryan Christ] Manage Apps: clicking a field in the Add/Edit form now
   focuses the field under the cursor -- the Params field was previously
   unreachable by mouse -- and Close warns about an unsaved category
   change before discarding it, matching the other tools.

*  [Bryan Christ] Consolidate the per-dialog popup primitives -- the
   warning, error, saved, confirm and file-load popups plus the listbox
   scroll callback -- into a shared manage_ui_common translation unit,
   removing several hundred lines of duplication across the Manage
   Settings / Hotkeys / Apps dialogs with no behaviour change.

*  [Bryan Christ] Popup consistency: the modal Manage Desktop / Print /
   Screenshot dialogs now dismiss when clicked outside, like the other
   popups.

Version 4.3.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Bump the minor version to mark dtach-based remote-session
   support as a complete, validated feature.  Detach with Ctrl-\ (or just
   drop the SSH connection) and vwm-resume reconnects, re-arming the mouse,
   cursor, keypad and a full repaint -- now including a same-size reattach
   and reattaching onto a running screensaver/lock, the two cases that
   previously came back needing a manual resize.  (Launchers and groundwork
   landed in 4.2.1; the reattach re-arm fixes in 4.2.2.)

*  [Bryan Christ] Docs: README tip for an /etc/update-motd.d/ login reminder
   that announces a resumable vwm session.

Version 4.2.2

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix dtach reattach not re-arming the terminal on a
   same-size reattach.  dtach -r winch sends SIGWINCH on every attach,
   but ncurses only turns it into KEY_RESIZE when the dimensions
   changed, so reattaching at the same window size never ran the re-arm
   cascade -- the mouse stayed dead, the cursor visible and the arrow
   keys emitting escapes until a manual resize.  vwm now catches
   SIGWINCH itself (chaining ncurses' handler) and the clock poller
   forces a KEY_RESIZE when a SIGWINCH arrived with no size change.

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix dtach reattach not re-arming when a screensaver or
   lock (e.g. vlock) was running at reattach time.  The saver runs in a
   fullscreen vterm and poll_input swallows KEY_RESIZE while it is up,
   so the reattach re-arm was discarded and the terminal stayed
   half-dead after unlock until a manual resize.  The saver's close
   path now queues a KEY_RESIZE once it is gone so the cascade runs.

Version 4.2.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Add detach/reattach support for running vwm on remote
   servers.  Two launchers, installed alongside the vwm binary, wrap vwm
   in dtach so a session survives an SSH disconnect: vwm-start begins a
   new session (refusing to clobber a live one -- it points you at
   vwm-resume -- while offering to clean up a stale socket), and
   vwm-resume reattaches.  A reattach reaches vwm as KEY_RESIZE (dtach
   -r winch sends SIGWINCH), so the resize cascade re-arms the mouse
   (re-emitting the enable escapes the new -- or freshly `reset`'d --
   tty has lost) and, via libviper >= 5.1.1, forces a full repaint and
   re-hides the cursor.  Detach disables mouse reporting on the outer
   terminal from the launcher's exit trap, since dtach gives the inner
   process no detach hook.  Requires dtach at runtime and libvdk
   (libviper) >= 5.1.1.

*  [Bryan Christ] Refactor: fold the startup / teleport / resize input
   re-arm (vk_kmio_init + nodelay) into a single vwm_input_rearm helper.

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix a saved non-default wallpaper not appearing until
   the first terminal resize.  The startup sequence built the active
   desktop's wallpaper cache during the first refresh -- before
   settings_load supplied the saved pattern/color -- and the second
   refresh reused that default-themed cache.  Invalidate the wallpaper
   cache after settings load so the startup refresh rebuilds it from the
   saved settings.

Version 4.2.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix vwmterm window titles going blank after the user
   triggers an apps reload (Manage Apps > Save, or VWM > Reload Apps).
   vwm_programs_purge freed every USER-zone vwm_module_t out from under
   any running vwmterm window that pointed at one, leaving a dangling
   vwmterm_data->mod.  The next SELECT-mode exit would read mod->title
   from the freed slab -- typically zeroed by a later calloc -- and
   write the resulting empty string back as " %s " ("  "), visually a
   blank gap in the centered title bar.  Snapshot the formatted title
   into vwmterm_data->title at launch and restore from there, so the
   window owns its title for its entire lifetime.

*  [Bryan Christ] Reorder the menubar so "Apps" appears before "VWM".
   The Apps menu is reached more often than the VWM (file) menu, so
   it now sits at the left edge where the menu bar starts.

Version 4.1.1

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix wheel scrolling in the Manage Apps dialog.  The
   main dialog's mouse gate dropped everything but BUTTON1, so wheel
   events never reached the apps listbox -- only BUTTON1 clicks could
   move the selection.  Widen the gate to include BUTTON4/5 and route
   wheel hits inside the listbox frame to vk_listbox_set_prev / _next,
   matching the click path (commit pending dropdown edits, repopulate
   the category / terminal / visibility dropdowns for the new entry,
   set FOCUS_APP_LIST).  Wheel hits outside the listbox frame are still
   dropped.

Version 4.1.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Fix mouse going unresponsive over SSH, and dying after
   a mouse-aware app (mc, vim) was opened in a vwmterm.  vwm marks its
   vterms VTERM_FLAG_EXTMOUSE so libvterm no longer seizes ncurses'
   mousemask out from under vk_kmio's SGR mouse parser.  Pairs with the
   in-libvdk SGR decode rewrite.  Requires libvterm >= 10.1 and
   libvdk (libviper) >= 5.1.0.

Version 4.0.0

*  [Bryan Christ] Per-desktop wallpaper patterns in Settings (None,
   Stiple, Small Bricks, Large Bricks, Dots 1, Dots 2).  Each desktop
   gets its own "Desktop N Wallpaper" row alongside the color row.
   Render dispatcher in bkgd.c; brick patterns fall back to Stiple at
   render time on non-UTF-8 terminals.
*  [Bryan Christ] Persist per-desktop colors and wallpapers to the
   JSON config (settings.desktop_colors, settings.desktop_wallpapers)
   so picks survive across restarts.  Sized to VWM_MAX_DESKTOPS so
   shrinking then growing surface_count preserves earlier picks.
*  [Bryan Christ] Port Edit App and Load Config popups to vk_popup_t.
*  [Bryan Christ] Add Manage Apps Menu dialog with app list, dropdown
   controls, edit/load popups, and mouse support.
*  [Bryan Christ] Add Teleport menu entry: prompts for a PTY path and
   migrates the active ncurses SCREEN to it via vk_screen_teleport.
   Recovers from teleport by re-arming colors, mouse mask (incl. hover
   tracking), and non-blocking input on the new SCREEN; queues a
   synthetic KEY_RESIZE so the panel + dialogs reflow to the
   destination's geometry.
*  [Bryan Christ] SIGTERM handler sets shutdown so the main loop
   unwinds through vk_screen_destroy -- the terminal is now restored
   instead of left in raw mode.
*  [Bryan Christ] Clock driver polls TIOCGWINSZ on the active SCREEN's
   fd every tick.  After teleport, SIGWINCH no longer reaches vwm
   (controlling TTY is still the launch terminal); the poll catches
   destination-PTY resizes and ungetches KEY_RESIZE.
*  [Bryan Christ] Per-desktop Settings: "Desktop N Color" rows
   dynamically populate from surface_count.  Each opens a vk_color
   picker (2x8, single-line dividers, 1x1 cells); on Save, each row's
   choice writes to vwm->desktop_color[N] and the wallpaper refreshes.

